/// Chapter 2 of the guide, written from md-docs/02-getting-started.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page GettingStartedGuide(path: "/docs/guide/getting-started", title: "Getting started", description: "Install wf, check the install, create a first project, run the dev server and make a change. Then set up the language server and read the build.", layout: DocsShell("Getting started", chapter: t("nav.group.start"), blurb: "Install the compiler, make a project, run it, change it and see the change — then set up your editor, or WebFluent Studio, and see what a build writes.", source: "md-docs/02-getting-started.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "install", label: "Install"), Anchor(id: "check-the-install", label: "Check the install"), Anchor(id: "create-a-project", label: "Create a project"), Anchor(id: "run-the-dev-server", label: "Run the dev server"), Anchor(id: "make-a-change", label: "Make a change"), Anchor(id: "set-up-your-editor", label: "Set up your editor"), Anchor(id: "change-how-wf-is-installed", label: "Change how wf is installed"), Anchor(id: "choose-a-template", label: "Choose a template"), Anchor(id: "write-a-site-from-scratch", label: "Write a site from scratch"), Anchor(id: "what-a-build-produces", label: "What a build produces"), Anchor(id: "the-project-in-brief", label: "The project, in brief")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/introduction", prevLabel: t("ch.01"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/tutorial", nextLabel: t("ch.03"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("In about ten minutes you install the compiler, create a project, run it in your browser, change one line and see the page follow. The rest of the chapter covers optional set-up: your editor, the other project templates, a site written from scratch, and what a build writes.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You need:\n\n- a terminal: macOS or Linux with `curl` and `bash`, or Windows with PowerShell (or, on any system, Rust's `cargo`);\n- a web browser;\n- a text editor. Any editor works; [Set up your editor](#set-up-your-editor) adds checks as you type, and [WebFluent Studio](#webfluent-studio) is an app made for WebFluent.")

        Heading("Install", id: "install").h2

        Markdown("WebFluent is one binary, `wf`. Run the line for your machine. Each one leaves `wf` on your `PATH`.")

        Heading("macOS and Linux", id: "macos-and-linux").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash")
            ])

        Markdown("The script fetches the binary for your platform from the latest release. You do not need Rust. It covers Apple Silicon and Intel Macs and x86_64 Linux.")

        Heading("Windows (PowerShell)", id: "windows-powershell").h3

        CodeBlock(#"irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.ps1 | iex"#, title: "powershell")

        Callout("On Windows, type `webfluent` wherever this guide types `wf`.", kind: .important)

        Markdown("Windows already has a `wf`: `C:\\Windows\\System32\\WF.msc`, the Windows Firewall console. System32 comes first on `PATH`, and `.MSC` is one of the extensions Windows tries for a bare name, so typing `wf` opens the firewall instead of WebFluent. For that reason the release zip, `install.ps1` and the `.msi` install the compiler as `webfluent.exe` beside `wf.exe`, and the `.msi` puts their folder on `PATH`. `webfluent build`, `webfluent serve` and `wf.exe build` all work. After `cargo install webfluent`, use `wf.exe`.")

        Heading("With Rust", id: "with-rust").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo install webfluent")
            ])

        Markdown("This builds `wf` from crates.io. It works everywhere Rust does, and it is how to install on Linux arm64, which has no prebuilt binary yet.")

        Heading("Packages", id: "packages").h3

        Markdown("Each [release](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/releases) also carries a `.deb`, an `.msi` and plain tarballs, for a machine where piping a script to a shell is not an option.")

        Heading("Check the install", id: "check-the-install").h2

        Markdown("Ask `wf` for its version:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf --version")
            ])

        Markdown("**You see** the release you installed:")

        CodeBlock(#"wf 5.11.0"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("If the shell says it cannot find `wf`, open a new terminal. The installer adds its directory to your `PATH` in your shell's startup file, and the terminal you ran it in read that file before the change.")

        Heading("Create a project", id: "create-a-project").h2

        Markdown("Make a project called `hello` and move into it:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf init hello"),
                Line(command: "cd hello")
            ])

        Markdown("**You see:**")

        CodeBlock(#"Created new WebFluent project: hello (template: spa)
  AGENTS.md is the language reference, for you and your coding agent"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("`wf init` writes a small application so you have something real to read: four pages (a dashboard, a task list, a profile and settings), three components and two stores, with tests under `tests/`.")

        Heading("Run the dev server", id: "run-the-dev-server").h2

        Markdown("Start the dev server:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf serve")
            ])

        Markdown("**You see:**")

        CodeBlock(#"Building hello...
  4 pages, 3 components, 2 stores
  Output: ./build/
Build complete.
Starting dev server at http://localhost:3000/"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Open `http://localhost:3000` in your browser. **You see** the app's **Dashboard** page: a navigation bar, three task counts and a list of recent tasks.\n\nLeave the server running. It rebuilds when you save a file, reloads the page when a build lands, and, when a build fails, draws the error over the page until a save fixes it.")

        Heading("Make a change", id: "make-a-change").h2

        Markdown("1. Open `src/pages/Home.wf` in your editor.\n2. Find the line `Text(\"Welcome to your dashboard.\").muted` and change the text to `Text(\"Welcome back.\").muted`.\n3. Save the file.\n\n**You see** the browser reload by itself, and the dashboard now reads **Welcome back.** under its heading.\n\nTo see what an error looks like, change `.muted` to `.mutd` and save. The page shows error `E103` over it, with the file, the line and the hint “Did you mean `.muted`?”. Change it back, save, and the page returns.\n\nYou have a working project. Stop the server with Ctrl+C when you are done. Run `wf build` to write the finished site to `build/`, ready for any static host ([Deploying](/docs/guide/deploying)).\n\nThe rest of this chapter is optional set-up. To keep learning, go to the [tutorial](/docs/guide/tutorial).")

        Heading("Set up your editor", id: "set-up-your-editor").h2

        Markdown("The language server, `wf-lsp`, gives your editor the same checks `wf build` runs, as you type: diagnostics, completion of props, flags and cases, hover with types and documentation, signature help, go to definition, references, rename, formatting, inlay types, semantic highlighting and quick fixes.\n\n`wf-lsp` is a second binary. Get it from the same [release](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/releases) as `wf` — `wf-lsp-<version>-<arch>-<os>.tar.gz` — and put it on your `PATH`, or build it from a clone of the repository:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo install --path crates/wf-lsp")
            ])

        Markdown("Then connect your editor:\n\n- **VS Code** — the extension, [webfluent-vscode](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/webfluent-vscode), brings highlighting and the language server. Until it is on the Marketplace, install the `.vsix` from its latest release with **Extensions: Install from VSIX**.\n- **Zed** — the extension, [webfluent-zed](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/webfluent-zed), brings Tree-sitter highlighting, the outline, snippets and the language server (found on `PATH`, or downloaded from the latest release). Install it with **zed: install dev extension**, pointed at a clone of it.\n- **Neovim, Helix, Emacs, or any other editor with LSP** — run `wf-lsp` over stdio for the `webfluent` file type (`*.wf`, `*.wfx`). [Editors](/docs/guide/cli#editors) has the configuration for each.")

        Heading("WebFluent Studio", id: "webfluent-studio").h3

        Markdown("[WebFluent Studio](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/webfluent-studio) is an app made for writing WebFluent. It puts a code editor, a live preview, a visual designer, Git and GitHub, this guide and an agent in one window. It does not use `wf-lsp`: the compiler runs inside the app, so it needs no `wf` on your `PATH`, no terminal and no dev server. A project built in Studio is byte-identical to the same project built with `wf build`, so you can move between Studio and the command line at any time.\n\n- **The preview** rebuilds as you type, and the build log and console link back to the source.\n- **Diagnostics** have the compiler's own message and its fix, one click away; **Explain** opens the chapter of this guide for that code.\n- **Design mode** edits the page on a canvas and writes the `.wf` you would have written.\n- **The agent** builds its own change before you see it, then you accept or reject it one hunk at a time.\n\nStudio is a separate, closed-source app at version 0.1, for Linux, macOS and Windows; Android follows. Its builds are published on [its releases page](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/webfluent-studio/releases). You do not need it to use WebFluent: `wf` and any editor are the whole toolchain.")

        Heading("Change how wf is installed", id: "change-how-wf-is-installed").h2

        Markdown("Both installers read two environment variables:")

        Table(caption: "Change how wf is installed") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Variable").header
                    Table.Cell("Effect").header
                    Table.Cell("Example").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`WF_INSTALL_DIR`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Installs the binary somewhere other than `~/.webfluent/bin`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`WF_INSTALL_DIR=/opt/wf/bin`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`WF_VERSION`") }
                    Table.Cell("Installs that release instead of the latest")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`WF_VERSION=v6.0.0`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Running the installer again upgrades `wf` in place.\n\nTo build from a clone instead — for an unreleased change, or to work on the compiler itself — run:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "git clone https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent.git"),
                Line(command: "cd WebFluent && cargo install --path .")
            ])

        Heading("Choose a template", id: "choose-a-template").h2

        Markdown("`wf init` takes a template with `-t`:")

        Table(caption: "Choose a template") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Template").header
                    Table.Cell("Makes").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`-t spa` (default)") }
                    Table.Cell("An interactive application, routed in the browser")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`-t static`") }
                    Table.Cell("A marketing site, every page pre-rendered to HTML")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`-t pdf`") }
                    Table.Cell("A PDF document")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`-t slides`") }
                    Table.Cell("A PDF slide deck")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("`--locales en,ar` starts an `spa` or `static` project translated: its text is in `src/translations/`, one file per language, and the first locale is the default ([Internationalisation](/docs/guide/i18n#start-a-project-translated)).\n\nIf you are unsure between `spa` and `static`, start with `static`. Every page is real HTML before any script runs, which is better for search and for the first paint, and the page is still fully interactive once the script loads. [Static or single-page](/docs/guide/static-and-spa) explains the difference.")

        Heading("Write a site from scratch", id: "write-a-site-from-scratch").h2

        Markdown("To see the smallest whole site, replace the contents of `src/` with two files, and delete the starter's tests under `tests/`, which test the pages you removed.\n\n`src/App.wf` is the shell every page shares, with the router in it:")

        CodeBlock(#"app {
    Navbar {
        Navbar.Brand { Text("Hello").heading }
        Navbar.Links {
            Link("Home", to: "/")
            Link("About", to: "/about")
        }
    }
    Router
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`src/pages/Home.wf` holds the pages, and each page names its own route:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "Hello", description: "A first WebFluent site.") {
    state count = 0
    Container {
        Heading("Hello, world").h1
        Text("You have clicked {count} times.")
        Button("Click me").primary { on click { count = count + 1 } }
    }
}

page About(path: "/about", title: "About", description: "What this site is for.") {
    Container {
        Heading("About").h1
        Paragraph("Two pages, one shell, no framework.")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Save, and the dev server shows a navigation bar with **Home** and **About**. **Click me** counts your clicks. Here is what each part does:\n\n- `app \{ … \}` is the shell: what every page sits inside. `Router` is where the current page renders. There is no route table; each `page` names its own `path:`.\n- `page Home(path: \"/\", title: …, description: …)` declares a page. `title` and `description` become the `<title>`, the meta description, the Open Graph and Twitter cards and the JSON-LD. If you leave them out, the build warns you.\n- `state count = 0` is a signal. `Text(\"… \{count\} …\")` reads it, so the text updates when it changes.\n- In `Button(\"Click me\").primary \{ on click \{ … \} \}`, the label is the one positional argument, `.primary` is a flag (a case of the button's `tone` prop), and the block holds the click handler.\n- `Container`, `Heading`, `Text`, `Paragraph`, `Navbar` and `Link` are built-ins. The [components reference](/docs/reference) lists all of them.")

        Heading("What a build produces", id: "what-a-build-produces").h2

        Markdown("`wf build` writes the finished site to `build/`. A static build with two pages and a `path: \"*\"` page writes files like these:")

        CodeBlock(#"build/
  index.html          the page, pre-rendered (static) or the app shell (spa)
  about/index.html    one directory per route in a static build
  404.html            the `path: "*"` page, which a host serves for unknown paths
  app.js              the runtime this program needs, its stores and components
  pages/Home.js       each page in its own chunk, loaded when its route shows
  pages/Home.css      the styles only that page reaches
  styles.css          tokens, the built-ins you used, your stylesheets
  sitemap.xml         every indexable route (needs meta.site_url)
  robots.txt
  _headers            the security headers (build.csp), for hosts that read the file
  sw.js               the service worker, when the config names "offline"
  *.gz                pre-compressed copies a host can serve as they are"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Do not edit anything in `build/`: the next build writes it again. Keep it out of version control; the `.gitignore` that `wf init` writes already does. The build copies `public/` to the root of `build/` as it is.\n\n`app.js` carries only the parts of the runtime the program reaches. The runtime is a set of feature modules — `each` for a `for`, `router` for a second page, `net` for a `resource`, `icons` for an `Icon` — and the build works out which ones the code it just wrote calls. A page of static text carries 2 of the 41 modules: an `app.js` of 6.8 kB gzipped. `wf build --stats` shows what a build weighs, and [Performance](/docs/guide/performance) explains how to read it.")

        Heading("The project, in brief", id: "the-project-in-brief").h2

        Markdown("A project's files are laid out like this:")

        CodeBlock(#"AGENTS.md             the language reference, for you and a coding agent
webfluent.app.json    the config
src/
  App.wf              the app shell (optional)
  pages/*.wf          pages
  components/*.wf     components
  stores/*.wf         stores
  *.css               your own stylesheets, bundled into styles.css
  translations/*.json translation files, with "i18n" in the config
public/               copied to the build's root (images, fonts, favicon)
tests/*.wf            `test "…" { }` declarations for `wf test`"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("The compiler reads every `.wf` or `.wfx` file under `src/` and merges them into one program, so a page in one file uses a component from another without importing it. The folders are a convention, not a rule. [Project structure](/docs/guide/project-structure) covers naming, organisation and the config, and the [configuration reference](/docs/guide/config) lists every key.\n\nThe config needs only a name:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "name": "hello" }"#, title: "json")
    }
}
